Lithium isotopes fractionate substantially during a wide variety of natural
processes,  [30]  including mineral formation (chemical precipitation), [
metabolism ](/wiki/Metabolism "Metabolism") , and [ ion exchange
](/wiki/Ion_exchange "Ion exchange") . Lithium ions substitute for [ magnesium
](/wiki/Magnesium "Magnesium") and iron in octahedral sites in [ clay
](/wiki/Clay "Clay") minerals, where  6  Li is preferred to  7  Li, resulting
in enrichment of the light isotope in processes of hyperfiltration and rock
alteration. The exotic  11  Li is known to exhibit a [ neutron halo
](/wiki/Halo_nucleus "Halo nucleus") , with 2 neutrons orbiting around its
nucleus of 3 protons and 6 neutrons. The process known as [ laser isotope
separation ](/wiki/Atomic_vapor_laser_isotope_separation "Atomic vapor laser
isotope separation") can be used to separate lithium isotopes, in particular
