[ M. Stanley Whittingham ](/wiki/M._Stanley_Whittingham "M. Stanley
Whittingham") conceived [ intercalation ](/wiki/Intercalation_\(chemistry\)
"Intercalation \(chemistry\)") electrodes in the 1970s and created the first
rechargeable lithium-ion battery, based on a [ titanium disulfide
](/wiki/Titanium_disulfide "Titanium disulfide") cathode and a lithium-
aluminum anode, although it suffered from safety problems and was never
commercialized.  [16]  [ John Goodenough ](/wiki/John_Goodenough "John
Goodenough") expanded on this work in 1980 by using [ lithium cobalt oxide
](/wiki/Lithium_cobalt_oxide "Lithium cobalt oxide") as a cathode.  [17]  The
first prototype of the modern Li-ion battery, which uses a carbonaceous anode
rather than lithium metal, was developed by [ Akira Yoshino
](/wiki/Akira_Yoshino "Akira Yoshino") in 1985 and commercialized by a [ Sony
](/wiki/Sony "Sony") and [ Asahi Kasei ](/wiki/Asahi_Kasei "Asahi Kasei") team
led by Yoshio Nishi in 1991.  [18]  [ M. Stanley Whittingham
](/wiki/M._Stanley_Whittingham "M. Stanley Whittingham") , [ John Goodenough
](/wiki/John_Goodenough "John Goodenough") , and [ Akira Yoshino
](/wiki/Akira_Yoshino "Akira Yoshino") were awarded the 2019 Nobel Prize in
Chemistry for their contributions to the development of lithium-ion batteries.

